# The build context is the REPO ROOT (docker/build.sh always passes it), and
# the Dockerfiles need almost none of it: two files to COPY, plus the docker/
# directory whose install scripts they bind-mount.
#
# AN ALLOWLIST RATHER THAN A DENYLIST, because what it keeps out is large and
# arrives by accident. Every `docker build` would otherwise ship .git, .venv,
# the sccache store and whatever local scratch sits under .temp/ to the daemon
# before running the first layer -- tens of megabytes on this box today, and
# whatever someone drops there tomorrow. An allowlist excludes the next big
# directory by default instead of relying on anyone remembering to add it.
*

!pyproject.toml
!uv.lock
# `uv sync` reads the project metadata, which names these two.
!README.md
!LICENSE
# Bind-mounted by RUN --mount=type=bind in Dockerfile.{cuda,hip,combined}: a
# bind source must be IN the context even though it is never COPYed.
!docker/
